00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:33,000
Scooby-Doo meets the Yogi Bear!

2
00:06:54,000 --> 00:07:00,000
Scooby-Doo! in Arabian Nights is a family, fantasy,
animation film directed by Jun Falkenstein.

3
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:27,000
Scooby-Doo and Shaggy travel to Arabia to become
the Caliph's Royal Food Tasters.

4
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:54,000
But they bite off more than they can chew and are forced to run for their lives!

5
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:21,000
It's a wild magic carpet ride as Scooby-Doo, Shaggy and their genie (Yogi Bear) and a jolly sailor named Sinbad (Magilla
Gorilla) take you on an adventure of mistaken identities, exotic locations and fun-filled action and surprises!

6
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:48,000
Scooby Doo / Boo-Boo Bear (voice), played by Don Messick.

7
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:15,000
Shaggy Rogers (voice), played by Casey Kasem.

8
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:42,000
Aliyah-Din (voice), played by Jennifer Hale.

9
00:31:03,000 --> 00:31:09,000
Additional Voices (voice), played by Nick Jameson.

10
00:34:30,000 --> 00:34:36,000
Additional Voices (voice), played by Tony Jay.

11
00:37:57,000 --> 00:38:03,000
Yogi Bear / Royal Chef (voice), played by Greg Burson.

12
00:41:24,000 --> 00:41:30,000
Scooby-Doo in Arabian Nights (also known as Arabian Nights) is a 1994 made-for-television film
produced by Hanna-Barbera Cartoons, and aired on syndication on September 3, 1994.

13
00:44:51,000 --> 00:44:57,000
It is an adaptation of The Book of One Thousand and One Nights and features
appearances by Scooby-Doo and Shaggy Rogers, in wrap around segments.

14
00:48:18,000 --> 00:48:24,000
The bulk of the special is devoted to two tales, one featuring Yogi Bear
and Boo-Boo Bear, and the other starring Magilla Gorilla.

15
00:51:45,000 --> 00:51:51,000
It is animated with bright colors, stylized character designs and a more flat style compared to the previous television films, and
musically scored by veteran animation composer Steven Bernstein, showing strong influence from the high-budget Warner Bros.

16
00:55:12,000 --> 00:55:18,000
Animation and Steven Spielberg cartoons of that era,
Tiny Toon Adventures and Animaniacs.

17
00:58:39,000 --> 00:58:45,000
This would prove to be the last film in which Don Messick voices Scooby-Doo and Boo Boo before his retirement in 1996 (though he would voice Scooby-Doo one more
time in the Scooby-Doo Mystery video game), and the last in which Allan Melvin voices Magilla Gorilla (as well as his last film role overall).

18
01:02:06,000 --> 01:02:12,000
The film explores themes of europe, europe journey, talking
dog, criminal investigation, woman director.

19
01:05:33,000 --> 01:05:39,000
The tone is lighthearted and mind_bending.
